<%- include('partials/header', {title: 'Amazon EFS Shared Storage'}) %>

<section class="project-hero">
  <a href="/" class="project-back-btn"><i class="fas fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to Portfolio</a>
  <h1>Amazon EFS Shared Storage & Performance Testing</h1>
  <p>Created an Amazon EFS file system, mounted to EC2, benchmarked throughput with FIO, and monitored metrics in CloudWatch.</p>
  <div class="project-hero-tags">
    <span>EFS</span><span>EC2</span><span>NFS</span><span>CloudWatch</span><span>FIO Benchmark</span>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="project-details">
  <div class="center">
    <img src="/images/efs-storage.png" alt="EFS Storage Architecture" class="project-diagram">
  </div>

  <div class="project-section">
    <h3>Project Overview</h3>
    <p>This project demonstrates <strong>Amazon Elastic File System (EFS)</strong> — a scalable, fully managed NFS file system shareable across multiple EC2 instances.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Created <strong>Security Groups</strong> allowing NFS traffic (port 2049).</li>
      <li>Created an <strong>EFS file system</strong> with mount targets in each AZ.</li>
      <li>Connected to EC2 via <strong>Session Manager</strong> (no SSH keys needed).</li>
      <li>Mounted EFS using <strong>NFS4 protocol</strong> with amazon-efs-utils.</li>
      <li>Tested performance using <strong>FIO</strong> with 10GB writes, 200 concurrent ops.</li>
      <li>Monitored <strong>CloudWatch metrics</strong> for throughput analysis.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section alt-bg">
    <h3>Architecture Explanation</h3>
    <div class="architecture-grid">
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>🗂️ EFS Layer</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>EFS File System</strong> — Scalable NFS storage</li>
          <li><strong>Mount Targets</strong> — Access point per AZ</li>
          <li>Auto grows/shrinks as needed</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>🔒 Security</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>EFS Mount Target SG</strong> — Allows port 2049</li>
          <li><strong>EFS Client SG</strong> — Attached to EC2</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>📊 Monitoring</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>CloudWatch</strong> — PermittedThroughput, DataWriteIOBytes</li>
          <li><strong>FIO</strong> — Benchmarking tool</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section">
    <h3>Implementation Steps</h3>
    <div class="steps-grid">
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><p>Created <strong>Security Groups</strong> for EFS mount target (port 2049) and EC2 client.</p></div>
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><p>Created <strong>EFS file system</strong> with mount targets in all AZs.</p></div>
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><p>Installed <strong>amazon-efs-utils</strong> and mounted EFS: <code>sudo mount -t nfs4 ...</code></p></div>
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><p>Ran <strong>FIO benchmark</strong> — 10GB file, 200 concurrent write operations.</p></div>
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">5</div><p>Monitored <strong>CloudWatch</strong> for PermittedThroughput and write IOPS metrics.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section alt-bg">
    <h3>Key Learnings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Created <strong>EFS file system</strong> with cross-AZ mount targets.</li>
      <li>Used <strong>Session Manager</strong> for secure EC2 access without SSH keys.</li>
      <li>Performed <strong>performance testing</strong> using FIO benchmarking tool.</li>
      <li>Understood EFS <strong>burst throughput</strong> and permitted throughput limits.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="center">
    <a href="/" class="btn-back"><i class="fas fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to Portfolio</a>
  </div>
</div>

